      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that by replacing $a_{11}$, $a_{34}$, and $a_{41}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 2, 3, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. The other options will not give us a matrix in row echelon form.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5c91a225-578f-5858-a60b-4bf7eb6e5122",
